## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 3.0 named *which levers* exist (mechanism). Part 3.1 named *which compounds* pull them (lineage). Part 3.2 is the **third coordinate: the WHEN and HOW MUCH** — the article's own framing. This is where "lowest effective dose, multi-pathway, don't waste blasts" stops being a philosophy and becomes **a calendar**. The article even hands us the hero: a section literally titled *"The enhanced-longevity year on one page"* with a five-row phase table. The poster is that one page, drawn as an instrument.

The controlling insight — the thing a reader must *feel* instantly — is **restraint expressed as timing.** The whole year is a loop you ride: **push to "calculated unhealthiness," then deliberately pull back to recover**, on repeat. Cycle design is not a dose you pick; it's a *rhythm you keep*. Three structural truths sit under that:

1. **Two stacked periodizations, not one.** A **drug calendar** (~8 months "On" / 3–4 months "Down") runs on *top* of a slower **food calendar** (lean-bulk ~75% / cut ~20% / maintenance ~5%, each with refeeds and mini-cuts inside it). The food is the lever you actually pull most often; the drugs are the lever of last resort. The poster must show them as **two coupled tracks on the same time axis**, not a single bar.

2. **Clearing a cycle ≠ restoring your health.** The single most counter-intuitive number in the article: a 4-week break is a *clean-out*, not a recovery. It takes ~4–6 weeks just for the last cycle to **clear**, and longer for lipids/liver/kidney/fertility to **normalise** — so a *real* down-period is **3–4 months**. The visual must make this true by **proportional length**: the down-period is a genuine quarter of the ring, not a token sliver. Drawing it short *is* the lie the article is correcting.

3. **The body-fat gate is the rule the whole loop runs on.** ==Never run a surplus above ~15% body fat.== Grow in the **8–12%** window, pull back with a cut at **~15–17%**. Past the gate, insulin sensitivity craters and the surplus *hinders* the very muscle the drugs are paying for ("the offseason is not a free-for-all" / "Michelin Man"). This is the one hard line — and the natural home for the poster's single red.

And the discipline that governs escalation: **the adjustment ladder — food → training → dose.** Drugs are the *last* rung, not the first. "Milk the dose" — squeeze the living soul out of your current dose before adding to it. When you finally add a compound: **one at a time, with bloods 4 weeks in**, bioidentical-first (the Golden Triangle: Test → GH → Insulin), exotics last.

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** _Cycle design is a calendar of restraint. You ride a loop — prepare, blast, cut, then a real 3–4-month recovery — keeping the body fat inside its gate and reaching for the dose only after food and training are exhausted. The skill isn't the stack you run; it's the discipline of when you push and when you pull back._

This is the concept a generic chat would miss. It would draw a single "cycle bar" (load goes up, load goes down) or a stock periodization wave. The *real* payload is **the coupled drug/food tracks on one year axis, the down-period drawn long enough to be honest, and the body-fat trace caged inside its gate** — with the escalation discipline (food→training→dose) as a separate decision instrument off the timeline.

