## 1. The Big Idea

_The enhanced year is a loop you ride, not a dose you pick. One time axis carries two coupled schedules — the **drugs** on top (~8 mo on / 3–4 mo down) and the **food** underneath (bulk / cut / maintenance) — while a single body-fat trace snakes through both, **caged between a floor and a ceiling it must never breach.** The poster makes "cycle design" mean **timing and restraint**: push to a monitored peak, then pull all the way back to a recovery long enough to actually restore the vessel. A planning instrument a reader studies to lay out a year before buying anything — not a protocol to copy, not a motivational image._

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure

An **annual operating schedule / forward mission-plan** — the visual language of an engineering project Gantt fused with a control-envelope trace. It earns its place four ways:

1. **The article is literally a calendar.** Its climax is *"the enhanced-longevity year on one page."* A horizontal **year axis** with stacked, time-aligned lanes is the honest shape of "when and how much" — and it's a sub-archetype the Fit set has never used (the planned *future* schedule, vs 2.1's recorded *past* telemetry).
2. **The two coupled tracks draw "periodise the food, not just the drugs" for free.** A **PHARMACOLOGY lane** (Preparation → Blast → Cut → Cruise/Down) sitting directly above a **FOOD lane** (lean-bulk / cut / maintenance with refeed + mini-cut ticks), vertically aligned on the same months, shows the hand-off the article describes: food is pulled often, the drug lever only when food turns to fat.
3. **The down-period draws "clearing ≠ restoring" by length.** Render the 3–4-month Down at *true proportional width* (a real quarter of the year). A faint ghosted "4-week token break" stub beside it, amber-flagged `clean-out ≠ recovery`, makes the correction visible as a length difference, not a caption.
4. **The body-fat trace draws the gate rule by geometry.** One continuous trace runs through both phases, **bounded by two horizontal gate lines** — a green-ok push band (8–12%) and a hard ceiling (~15%). When the trace tries to climb past the ceiling during a sloppy surplus, it enters the **one red zone** on the poster. "Never surplus above 15%" becomes a wall the line hits, not a sentence.

The spine tying it together is the **four-phase loop** — Preparation → Blast → Cut → Cruise/Down — drawn so the reader sees it *returns to the start* (push → pull back → repeat). Off the timeline, two compact decision instruments: the **adjustment ladder** (a vertical 3-rung escalator, food→training→dose, drugs at the top as last resort) and the **diagnostic checkpoint flags** (3-month blood draws pinned onto the year axis). A small **wet/dry tuning dial** grace-note carries the article's sharp reframe ("wetness is a setting, not a property — DHT is the dial"). The **80% retention** mark lands at the deflation hand-off out of the Cut.

This is a deliberately *different instrument* from the prior PE posters but lives in the same locked "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument" language — and it is the natural *closer* to the Optimize arc: 3.0 mapped mechanism, 3.1 mapped lineage, **3.2 maps time.**

