## 5. UX / visual encoding expectations _(semantic — colors come from design.md, not from here)_

- **The YEAR AXIS + the two coupled lanes are the load-bearing objects.** The axis must be the boldest horizontal element; the pharmacology lane and food lane must read as *vertically aligned to the same months* so the hand-off (drugs on top, food underneath) is pre-attentive. If a reader takes only the axis and two lanes, they should still get "a year ridden as a planned loop, drugs over food."
- **Honest proportional width is the argument.** The **Down-period block must be drawn at a true ~quarter width** and the bulk at ~75% of the food lane — the lengths *are* the teaching. Never compress the down-period to a sliver "to fit"; that reproduces the exact mistake the article corrects. The ghost 4-week stub beside it must read visibly shorter.
- **The body-fat trace carries the gate rule by position, not hue.** Encode it as a continuous line bounded by two horizontal reference lines; the meaning lives in *where the line sits* (inside band = ok, above ceiling = breach). It must survive grayscale — a reader should see the breach as the line crossing a wall even with color stripped.
- **Red is rationed to exactly ONE object: the body-fat ceiling breach (the Michelin-Man zone).** It is the one hard "never" in the article and the most common expensive mistake. Everything else cautionary — the ghost token-break, the prolactin/cortisol note, the "exotics last" gate, calculated-unhealthiness — stays **amber/caution** or neutral instrument ink. (Consistent with the whole PE set: ~1 red object, reserved for the one real failure-rule.)
- **The loop must read as a loop.** The return-arrow from Down → Preparation is essential: this is "push then pull back, repeat," not a one-shot bar. Without the closing arrow the poster reads as a single linear cycle and loses the longevity thesis.
- **The adjustment ladder reads bottom→top with drugs at the TOP.** Drugs being the *last/highest* rung is the point; food at the base, dose at the crown, gates between. Don't let the ladder imply drugs-first by putting them low or large.
- **Success/green is used for the gate band and the in-range trace; danger is the single breach; amber is everything else cautionary.** This three-state discipline matches 3.1 and the PE set — green = the envelope you keep, red = the one rule you can't break, amber = the judgement calls.
- **Hierarchy of attention:** headline → the year axis + two lanes + body-fat trace (hero) → the long down-period block + loop arrow → the red ceiling-breach → the adjustment ladder → the blood-flag checkpoints → wet/dry dial + 80% mark + footer (quiet chrome). Within the lanes: phase/segment names loudest, the proportion + duration numbers supporting, the refeed/mini-cut ticks fine detail.
- **Restraint = the mood.** A precision project schedule, not a "transformation timeline." Dark instrument ground, mono month-labels and tick-marks, ruler-drawn phase blocks and the gate lines, generous negative space between lanes. Color rationed to the green gate band, the one red breach, and amber cautions.
- **Saveability:** shape/position carry the meaning (block widths = durations, lane alignment = coupling, trace height vs gate lines = the body-fat rule, ladder order = escalation) so the schedule parses on a phone and in grayscale — a reader should still see "long recovery, food under drugs, trace caged, drugs last" with color stripped.

