## 7. What to Avoid

1. **A single "cycle bar" or a generic periodization wave (load ramps up, load ramps down).** That is the obvious move and it throws away the article's actual thesis. The upgrade is the **two coupled tracks on one year axis** (drugs over food) plus the **body-fat trace caged in a gate** — the poster exists to show that cycle design is a *coupled, gated, looping* schedule, not a dose curve.
2. **Compressing the down-period to a thin sliver "to make it fit."** This reproduces the exact mistake the article is correcting (a 4-week break treated as recovery). The 3–4-month Down must be drawn at a **true ~quarter width** — its honest length is the single most important proportion on the poster.
3. **A circular routine-clock / 12-spoke calendar wheel as the hero.** Tempting because "the year is a loop," but it collapses the two coupled tracks and the gate trace into a decorative ring and loses the proportional widths. Keep the loop as a *return-arrow on a horizontal schedule*, not a clock face. (Reserve circularity for the one small loop arrow, not the whole layout.)
4. **Putting drugs first / largest, or flooding the poster with red.** The adjustment ladder must read food→training→**dose-last**; drugs sit at the top rung. Red is one object only (the >15% body-fat ceiling breach); calculated-unhealthiness, the token-break, and the prolactin note are amber. And no copy-paste cycle and no doses on named anabolics beyond the article's conservative "lowest effective dose" illustrations — harm-reduction framing only.
